Title:
DLL Sideloading for Initial Access and Bypassing Elastic EDR

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This write-up demonstrates using DLL sideloading as an initial access method to execute a C2 beacon on a Windows 10 endpoint monitored by Elastic Security/Elastic EDR.  
- It focuses on identifying a signed, legitimate target binary (Sysinternals Process Monitor) that attempts to load a missing DLL from its own application directory, enabling a malicious replacement to be loaded first due to Windows DLL search order.  
- The author uses Procmon to find `NAME NOT FOUND` DLL load attempts and selects `PROPSYS.dll` as the sideload target.  
- A beacon DLL is generated with Adaptix Framework and configured with IAT hiding (empty import table) to reduce static indicators in the PE imports.  
- Execution occurs inside `Procmon64.exe`; the app may error due to missing exports, but the payload runs upon load.  
- In the lab scenario, Elastic did not generate an alert for the sideloaded DLL, highlighting detection gaps when code runs inside trusted signed processes with minimal static artifacts.  
- Useful for red teamers/pentesters validating initial access tradecraft and for defenders assessing DLL search order abuse exposure around admin tooling.

Technical Focus:
- Windows DLL search order / DLL sideloading (MITRE ATT&CK T1574.002)
- Procmon-based discovery of missing DLL loads (`NAME NOT FOUND` / `CreateFile`)
- Signed binary proxy execution (Sysinternals Procmon as host process)
- Beacon-as-DLL payload generation and execution on load
- Import Address Table (IAT) hiding / empty import table to reduce static detection
- Elastic EDR detection validation and observed blind spots

Use Cases:
- Identify sideloadable DLL opportunities in enterprise-installed software/tooling
- Achieve initial code execution within a trusted signed process for stealthier footholds
- Test/benchmark Elastic EDR (or other EDR) coverage against DLL search order hijacking
- Develop and validate payload configurations that minimize static PE indicators
- Blue team threat hunting and hardening around writable application directories

Keywords:
DLL sideloading, T1574.002, Windows DLL search order, Sysinternals, Process Monitor, Procmon64.exe, PROPSYS.dll, NAME NOT FOUND, CreateFile, signed binary proxy execution, Adaptix Framework, C2 beacon, DLL payload, IAT hiding, empty import table, Elastic Security, Elastic EDR, initial access, Windows 10 Enterprise, application directory hijack